I've written all five deliverables at the project root: `ARTICLE.md`, `RESEARCH_PAPER.md`, `RESEARCH_PAPER.tex`, `demo.py` and `PACKAGE.json`. `demo.py`, the algorithm code and the interactive widget's script all run and give the expected numbers. No LaTeX compiler was available here, so `RESEARCH_PAPER.tex` has not been compiled, and the two plotting scripts have not been run because matplotlib isn't installed. I did not rebuild the three Lean files either: they are copied into `PACKAGE.json` as they were.

**Framing change.** The documents don't simply repeat the round-30 verdict that "hints compound like capacities." They follow what the finished mathematics actually proves. The conditional sum hint is I(T;N,s) − I(T;N), the gap hint is I(T;N,d) − I(T;N), the joint hint value is I(T;s,d) − I(T;N), and synergy is the joint value minus the two single hints. With these definitions:
- **No sign:** synergy is exactly −1 on a two-sample example mod 5 and exactly +1 on a four-sample example mod 7.
- **One-Bit Law:** over one odd prime field, synergy is at most 1 − max(sum hint, gap hint), so at most 1 bit. It reaches 1 only when both single hints are zero.
- **One bit per field:** over a product of k odd prime fields, synergy is at most k bits. A 16-sample example over ℤ/7×ℤ/7 reaches exactly 2 bits. So the reported +1.40 bits is impossible over one prime field and possible over two.
- **Legendre Law:** a cell (N, s) with nonzero discriminant s² − 4N contains two factor pairs (the "orientation bit") exactly when the Legendre symbol of s² − 4N is +1. It follows that examples made entirely of square pairs p = q cannot compound.

The experimental figures (2.4291, 1.0288, and the flagged 0.9663-bit statistic) are presented only as reported measurements. The 0.9663 figure is explicitly left uninterpreted, with the suggested permutation test noted.

